\subsection{Unpickling without the garbage collector}
\label{sec:unpickle-without-gc}

Unpickling is the largest part of a load.
It creates a few million objects in one go, and none of them is garbage.
Python's cyclic garbage collector cannot know that: it runs whenever enough new
objects exist, and each run walks objects that a later run walks again.
On a real cache with a 72~MB pickle (92\,000 users in 176 courses), unpickling
took 2.1~s with the collector on and 0.8~s with it off, which took the whole
load from 2.9~s to 1.8~s.
So we keep the collector off while we unpickle.

We also tried [[gc.freeze]] after the load, which moves the loaded objects out
of the collector's sight for the rest of the run.
It made no measurable difference to a command, and in a long-lived program it
would keep every graph it ever loaded alive, so we leave it out.

Afterwards we restore the state we found, we don't turn the collector on.
This loader also runs inside other programs, through
[[canvaslms.get_canvas]], and one that runs with the collector off must find it
off when we return.
The [[finally]] matters for the same reason: a file that cannot be unpickled is
an ordinary outcome here, it gives a fresh [[Canvas]] object, and it must not
leave the rest of the program without garbage collection.
<<functions>>=
@contextlib.contextmanager
def garbage_collection_paused():
  """Keep the cyclic garbage collector off inside the `with` block."""
  was_enabled = gc.isenabled()
  gc.disable()
  try:
    yield
  finally:
    if was_enabled:
      gc.enable()
@

Let's verify that the collector is off exactly while we unpickle, whatever its
state before, and that a payload that cannot be unpickled restores it too.

