\subsection{Saving only when something was sent to Canvas}
\label{sec:skip-unchanged-save}

A command that answers from the cache alone used to cost about as much as one
that fetched: with a 96~MB cache file, [[courses]] for one course took 14~s, of
which loading the cache took 5~s and saving it again 5~s (issue \#586).
The save wrote back the bytes it had read, re-encrypted.

\paragraph{Where the time goes.}
To see what skipping the save could gain, we built a cache of the same size
offline: 262 courses with 10 assignments and 60 students each, fetched through
the real wrappers from a stand-in Canvas, which gives a 69~MiB pickle and a
92~MiB encrypted file.
\Cref{tab:cache-save-load-times} shows how long each step took.
The machine was busy, so the seconds are about five times those of the issue;
the shares are what we go by.
\begin{table}
\centering
\begin{tabular}{lrl}
\toprule
Step & Time & Share \\
\midrule
Pickle & 15.3~s & 71\,\% of the save \\
Encrypt & 6.2~s & 29\,\% of the save \\
Write (with [[fsync]]) & 0.1~s & under 1\,\% of the save \\
\midrule
Read & 0.2~s & under 1\,\% of the load \\
Decrypt & 5.4~s & 19\,\% of the load \\
Unpickle & 22.2~s & 80\,\% of the load \\
\midrule
Key derivation & 0.2~s & once for each \\
SHA-256 of the pickle & 1.8~s & \\
BLAKE2b of the pickle & 0.7~s & \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Time spent saving and loading a synthetic cache with a 69~MiB pickle,
step by step.}
\label{tab:cache-save-load-times}
\end{table}

\paragraph{Two ways to tell that nothing changed.}
The first is to compare contents: pickle the object as before, and compare a
digest of the bytes with one taken of the decrypted bytes at load.
It cannot be wrong, since equal bytes are an equal cache, but it still pays for
the pickling, which is 71\,\% of the save, and the digest itself; it saves about
a quarter of the save.
It gains even less than that: a freshly fetched object graph does not pickle to
the bytes it pickles to once reloaded (fresh objects share strings that
unpickled ones do not), so the first command after each one that fetched would
save again.
We measured that later round trips are byte for byte stable.

The second is to keep a flag that says the cache changed, and skip the whole
save, pickling included, when it is not set.
Its risk is a place that changes the cache without setting the flag: the
change is lost, silently.
Setting the flag in each mechanism that stores something, the wrappers, the
[[LazySubmission]] refresh, the invalidations on write and so on, would make
that risk grow with every mechanism someone adds later.

\paragraph{One place to set the flag: the request.}
We use the flag, but set it in one place.
Everything in the cache came from Canvas, so the cache learns something only
by asking Canvas: a fetch that stores, a refresh of a stale submission, a
refresh on a miss or on [[--refresh-cache]], a 404 that drops an entry, and a
write that is followed by an invalidation all send a request first.
We therefore count the requests, and save only when the count has changed since
the object was loaded.

What changes without a request is worth losing.
The time to live in [[outdated]] clears the timestamp of an expired listing,
[[LazySubmission.ensure_attrs]] fills in missing attributes with [[None]], and
commands decorate objects for their own output.
The next run recomputes each of them from what the file holds, since time only
moves forward and the file is the same.

We count each request when it is sent, not when it succeeds, because a failed
request changes the cache too: an expired course listing that Canvas did not
answer is given a new timestamp, so that the next command does not wait for
Canvas again (\cref{sec:course-listing-outage}).

\paragraph{Where to count.}
Every request [[canvasapi]] makes goes through [[Requester.request]], and so do
the requests our own code sends directly, for instance for New Quizzes.
Wrapping that method is one site for all of them, and a mechanism added later
is covered without anyone having to remember this section.
The few requests that bypass it are plain [[requests.get]] downloads of files,
which do not store anything in the Canvas object.

We find the count through [[Requester.request]] itself, as an attribute of
whatever function is there, rather than in a module variable.
Re-importing this module (as tests may) then neither wraps the method twice nor
leaves us reading a counter that the wrapper no longer increments.

That makes us depend on what others do to [[Requester.request]] after us, and a
wrong count fails the dangerous way: a count that stands still makes every later
save look unneeded, and what was fetched is lost.
Two cases matter.
Someone may wrap the method again with [[functools.wraps]], which copies the
attributes of our wrapper onto theirs; a plain number would be copied once and
then stand still on the outer function, while ours counted on.
So the attribute is a one-element list, the counter's cell, which the copy shares
instead of duplicating.
Someone may also wrap it without [[functools.wraps]], so that the attribute is
gone; we then do not know the count, [[requests_sent]] says [[None]], and
[[unchanged_since_stored]] below takes that as a reason to save.

The count is for the whole process and all [[Canvas]] objects; a request for one
object makes us save any other, which errs on the side of saving.
<<functions>>=
def count_requests(request):
  """Return `request` wrapped to count the requests it sends."""
  sent = [0]

  @functools.wraps(request)
  def counted_request(*args, **kwargs):
    sent[0] += 1
    return request(*args, **kwargs)

  counted_request.requests_sent = sent
  return counted_request

def requests_sent():
  """Return the number of requests sent to Canvas by this process so far.

  Returns None when the count cannot be found, because `Requester.request`
  was replaced by something that does not carry it.
  """
  sent = getattr(canvasapi.requester.Requester.request, "requests_sent", None)
  if isinstance(sent, list):
    return sent[0]
  return None
<<count the requests sent to Canvas>>=
if not hasattr(canvasapi.requester.Requester.request, "requests_sent"):
  canvasapi.requester.Requester.request = count_requests(
    canvasapi.requester.Requester.request)
@

\paragraph{What the file holds.}
For each [[Canvas]] object we remember the count at the moment it matched the
file: when it was loaded, and again after each save, since the package-level
[[canvaslms.save_canvas]] may save before the save at exit.
A [[Canvas]] object we never loaded, one created because there was no usable
cache file, is not remembered and is always saved, as it must replace that file.

The record lives in this module and not on the object, since anything on the
object would be pickled into the file that older copies of this library share.
It holds the objects weakly, so that a program loading the cache repeatedly does
not keep each old copy alive.
An object that cannot be referenced weakly, such as the dictionaries that stand
in for a [[Canvas]] in some tests, is not remembered either, and so is saved:
when we cannot tell, we save.
The same goes for an unknown count: two unknown counts must not compare equal.
<<functions>>=
def remember_as_stored(canvas):
  """Note that `canvas` matches the cache file as of the requests sent so far."""
  try:
    stored_at_request[canvas] = requests_sent()
  except TypeError:
    pass

def unchanged_since_stored(canvas):
  """Return True if no request was sent since `canvas` matched the file."""
  sent = requests_sent()
  if sent is None:
    return False
  try:
    return stored_at_request.get(canvas) == sent
  except TypeError:
    return False
<<constants>>=
stored_at_request = weakref.WeakKeyDictionary()
@

A program that changes the object by hand, without a request, would lose that
change, so [[save_canvas_cache]] takes [[force]] for it.
The tests that age a listing in the file to set up a scenario are such callers.
The check comes before the key derivation, so a skipped save costs nothing.
One consequence is that the modification time of the cache file now tells when
something was last fetched, not when the cache was last used.
<<skip the save if nothing was sent to Canvas>>=
if not force and unchanged_since_stored(canvas):
  if not not_yet_excluded(canvas):
    logger.info("Canvas cache save skipped: no request sent to Canvas since "
                "the cache was loaded or saved")
    return
  logger.info(f"Canvas cache: saving to leave "
              f"{', '.join(sorted(not_yet_excluded(canvas)))} out of the file")
