\subsection{Leaving the recent responses out of the file}

The [[Requester]] that [[canvasapi]] attaches to every object keeps the last
five responses it received in its [[_cache]] attribute.
Neither [[canvasapi]] nor we ever read them again, but they are part of the
object graph, so every cache file carried five [[Response]] objects with their
full bodies and the requests that produced them.
In the synthetic cache of \cref{sec:skip-unchanged-save} these are 250~kB of
bodies, yet leaving them out makes the pickle 2.8~MB smaller, from 72.7 to
69.9~MB, and the file 3.5~MiB, from 92.5 to 88.9~MiB, or 4\,\%.
The rest is how [[pickle]] refers to an object it has already written: by its
index in a memo, in 2 bytes for the first 256 objects and in 5 bytes after
that.
The requester comes early in the graph, so its responses took about 260 of the
cheap places from objects that are referred to again and again.
In a cache a sixth of that size, 134\,000 references became 3 bytes longer.
The time to save and load should drop in proportion, a few per cent, which is
less than the noise of our measurements.

We leave them out by giving [[Requester]] a [[__getstate__]], the hook
[[pickle]] asks for the state to store.
It returns the requester's attributes with [[_cache]] replaced by an empty
list.
Replaced and not removed, because of the copies of this library that share the
file: unpickling sets the stored attributes without running [[__init__]], and
their [[Requester.request]] starts with [[len(self._cache)]].
A requester without the attribute would fail at its first request, while one
with an empty list is exactly what [[__init__]] would have made.
Nothing else changes in the layout, so the schema version stays the same.
In the other direction, a file written by an older copy still has the
responses; we load them as before, and our next save leaves them out.

The hook lives here, next to the request counter, rather than with the other
patches of [[canvasapi]] in [[hacks/canvasapi.nw]].
Pickling is this module's business: the cache file is the only place these
objects are pickled.
<<functions>>=
def requester_state(requester):
  """Return the state to pickle for a `Requester`, without recent responses."""
  state = requester.__dict__.copy()
  state["_cache"] = []
  return state
<<leave the recent responses out of the pickle>>=
canvasapi.requester.Requester.__getstate__ = requester_state
@

Let's verify that a saved cache holds no [[Response]] at all, that it still
answers offline, and that its requester still works the way an older copy's
code uses it.
We look into the file by unpickling it with an [[Unpickler]] that records every
class the file names, since a [[Response]] could sit anywhere in the graph.

<<test functions>>=
def classes_in_cache_file(path):
  """Return the (module, name) of every class the cache file at path names."""
  pickled = Fernet(cache.derive_key("token", HOST)).decrypt(path.read_bytes())
  classes = set()

  class RecordingUnpickler(pickle.Unpickler):
    def find_class(self, module, name):
      classes.add((module, name))
      return super().find_class(module, name)

  RecordingUnpickler(io.BytesIO(pickled)).load()
  return classes

RESPONSE_CLASS = ("requests.models", "Response")

@responses.activate
def test_a_saved_cache_holds_no_responses(run_canvaslms, tmp_cache_path):
  responses.get(COURSES_URL, json=[OLD_COURSE])
  responses.get(f"{COURSES_URL}/1/assignments", json=[LAB])
  run_canvaslms("assignments", "-c", "Old")

  assert RESPONSE_CLASS not in classes_in_cache_file(tmp_cache_path)

  responses.reset()
  assert "Lab" in run_canvaslms("assignments", "-c", "Old")

