\subsubsection{Where the setting comes from}
\label{sec:cache-exclude-sources}

The kinds to exclude can be given in three places, and the most specific one
that says anything decides alone:
\begin{enumerate}
\item the option [[--cache-exclude]] of the command, or the argument
  [[cache_exclude]] of [[canvaslms.get_canvas]] in a program;
\item the environment variable [[CANVASLMS_CACHE_EXCLUDE]], if it is not
  empty and names only kinds that exist;
\item the key [[exclude]] under [[cache]] in the configuration file, a
  comma-separated string or a list:
\begin{minted}{json}
{"cache": {"exclude": "personal"}}
\end{minted}
\end{enumerate}
The order is that of the credentials, where the environment also goes before
the configuration file.
One source replaces the others, it does not add to them, so that
[[--cache-exclude none]] can turn off what the configuration file turned on.

The configuration file is the place for it when the choice is about the
machine and not about one command.
It is also the only one of the three that every program sharing the cache
file reads, since they all load through [[load_configured_canvas]], and an
exclusion is only as good as the least careful program that writes the file
(\cref{sec:cache-exclude-in-file}).

The environment variable was read when [[hacks/canvasapi.nw]] was imported.
Here we only decide whether one of the other two replaces it.

Replacing must never make the file hold more than its user meant, so the
environment gets to overrule the configuration file only when it says
something we understand.
An empty value says nothing; a variable can end up empty by accident, as in
[[CANVASLMS_CACHE_EXCLUDE=$UNSET]] in a crontab, and it must not undo the
configuration file.
A value with a name that is no kind is a mistake, reported with the other
malformed settings, which [[-q]] silences.
If it replaced the configuration file, a misspelling would quietly turn the
protection off, so then both hold: the kinds of the configuration file are
left out as well as those of the environment.
To exclude nothing on purpose, the environment says [[none]].
<<decide which kinds the cache file leaves out>>=
if cache_exclude is not None:
  set_cache_exclusion(cache_exclude, "cache_exclude")
else:
  hacks = canvaslms.hacks.canvasapi
  in_environment = hacks.env_names("CANVASLMS_CACHE_EXCLUDE",
                                   hacks.KIND_ALIASES)
  understood = (os.environ.get("CANVASLMS_CACHE_EXCLUDE", "").strip()
                and not hacks.unknown_kinds(in_environment))
  configured = config.get("cache")
  if (not understood and isinstance(configured, dict)
      and configured.get("exclude") is not None):
    set_cache_exclusion(configured["exclude"],
                        "cache.exclude in the configuration file")
    hacks.exclude_from_cache_file(excluded_kinds() | in_environment)
@

A name that is no kind is as dangerous here as in the environment: the user
believes something is left out that is not.
By now logging is configured, so we can warn right away.
A value that is neither a string nor a list of strings gets the same
treatment; we do not guess what a number was meant to exclude.
<<functions>>=
def set_cache_exclusion(kinds, setting):
  """Leave `kinds` out of the cache file, as `setting` asks.

  Warns about names that are no kind that is cached, and about a value that is
  no string or list of strings, which is ignored.
  """
  hacks = canvaslms.hacks.canvasapi
  if not (isinstance(kinds, str)
          or (isinstance(kinds, (list, tuple, set, frozenset))
              and all(isinstance(kind, str) for kind in kinds))):
    logger.warning(f"{setting}: ignoring {kinds!r}, which is neither a "
                   f"comma-separated string nor a list of kinds; "
                   f"{hacks.known_kinds_text()}")
    return
  unknown = hacks.exclude_from_cache_file(kinds)
  if unknown:
    logger.warning(f"{setting}: {hacks.unknown_kinds_message(unknown)}")
@

The command line can do better than a warning, since it can refuse to run.
[[kinds_to_exclude]] is the [[type]] that argparse applies to the option's
value: a misspelled kind ends the command with the usage and the list of
kinds, before anything is fetched or written.
<<functions>>=
def kinds_to_exclude(text):
  """Return `text`, the value of --cache-exclude, if it names only kinds."""
  hacks = canvaslms.hacks.canvasapi
  unknown = hacks.unknown_kinds(hacks.names_in(text, hacks.KIND_ALIASES))
  if unknown:
    raise argparse.ArgumentTypeError(
      f"unknown kind {', '.join(repr(kind) for kind in unknown)}; "
      f"{hacks.known_kinds_text()}")
  return text
@

