\section{The cache command and subcommands}

The [[cache]] command provides control over the various caches.
We provide subcommands for different cache management operations:
\begin{description}
\item[[cache clear]] Clears the encrypted Canvas object cache
\item[[cache clear-attachments]] Removes old cached attachment files
\end{description}

The command's description interpolates the live TTL values (imported above) so
the help always reflects the effective windows, and it names the
[[CANVASLMS_*]] environment variables that override them.  Surfacing the
override knobs here, next to the values they control, is the discoverable place
for a user wondering why the cache feels stale or stubborn.

\subsection{Laying out the help text}
\label{cache-help-layout}

The description contains lists: the time-to-live of each kind of object and the
environment variables.
argparse's default formatter treats the description as one running text and
joins every line, so the list items and the paragraphs around them came out as
a single block that is hard to read.
The contrast is [[RawDescriptionHelpFormatter]], which prints the description
exactly as written; the [[results]] command uses it for the same reason.
The price is that we wrap the text by hand to 80 columns, since the formatter
no longer does it.
The lines of the description are the \emph{interpolated} ones: a value such as
[[COURSE_CACHE_TTL_DAYS]] only adds a few characters, and the test below checks
that even three-digit values fit.

A list item is read as a sentence, so the unit must agree with the number:
[[1 day]] but [[2 days]].
Because the windows are user settings, 1 is a likely value, and it is the
default for the list of courses.
<<functions>>=
def count_unit(count, unit):
  """Returns `count` with `unit` in singular or plural, e.g. "1 day"."""
  return f"{count} {unit}" if count == 1 else f"{count} {unit}s"

def unused_courses_help():
  """Return the help paragraph on courses that have not been used for long."""
  if CACHE_KEEP_DAYS <= 0:
    return """\
The cache file keeps what is cached below a course however long ago it was
fetched, since CANVASLMS_CACHE_KEEP_DAYS is 0. A number of days makes the file
leave out the contents of a course when nothing below it has been fetched for
that long."""
  return f"""\
The cache file leaves out what is cached below a course once nothing below it
has been fetched for {count_unit(CACHE_KEEP_DAYS, "day")}; it is fetched again
when next read. The users who had left that course are then gone from the
cache, and a warning says so. CANVASLMS_CACHE_KEEP_DAYS sets the number of
days, and 0 keeps everything."""

def add_command(subp):
  """Adds the cache command to argparse parser"""
  cache_parser = subp.add_parser(
    "cache",
    help="Manage caches",
    formatter_class=argparse.RawDescriptionHelpFormatter,
    description=f"""
Manages the persistent caches used by canvaslms.

The Canvas object cache stores API responses (courses, assignments, users,
submissions) encrypted on disk. Objects are refreshed based on their type:

  - Submissions (non-passing): {count_unit(SUBMISSION_TTL_MINUTES, "minute")}
  - The list of courses: {count_unit(COURSE_CACHE_TTL_DAYS, "day")}
  - Users: {count_unit(USER_CACHE_TTL_DAYS, "day")}
  - Groups: {count_unit(GROUP_CACHE_TTL_DAYS, "day")}
  - Other objects: {count_unit(DEFAULT_CACHE_TTL_DAYS, "day")}

Submissions with passing grades (A, P, complete) don't expire. They are updated
when all submissions of the assignment are fetched, or by canvaslms
--refresh-cache.

The name of a course, user, group, assignment, quiz, page, module or file that
matches nothing also fetches that listing again, unless it is younger than
{count_unit(MISS_REFRESH_MIN_AGE_MINUTES, "minute")}. When more than one
listing may be fetched, a warning says how many; a quiz has two per course,
Classic and New Quizzes. For a user the rosters are fetched one at a time, in
the order of the courses, until one has the name. If
more than {MISS_REFRESH_MAX_LISTINGS} listings are older than that, or more than
{MISS_REFRESH_MAX_LISTINGS_ALL_COURSES} in a search over all courses (no -c, or
-c '.*'), none is fetched and a warning says so. With groups (-G, -C) the
names of the group and the category are looked up this way, the users in them
are not. To fetch what a command reads right away, use
canvaslms --refresh-cache; the rest of the cache is kept.

When the list of courses, or of a course's assignments, files or folders, is
fetched, what Canvas no longer returns is removed from the cache, unless it
returned nothing at all; a course only if Canvas, asked about it alone, answers
that it does not exist or that the user may not read it. Users who have left a
course are kept, since their results still count.

{unused_courses_help()}

Of each enrollment of a user, the cache file keeps the identifiers, the type,
the role, the state and the section. Users who have left a course keep all.

To keep student data out of the cache file, name the kinds of objects to leave
out: user, group and submission, or personal for all three. Give them with
canvaslms --cache-exclude KINDS, in CANVASLMS_CACHE_EXCLUDE, or in the
configuration file as {{"cache": {{"exclude": "personal"}}}}; the first of
these that is given decides, and none excludes nothing. Excluded objects are
only cached in memory during a run, so every run fetches them again. With
submissions excluded, downloaded attachments are not cached either; what is
already cached of them stays until clear-attachments removes it. Other
programs that share the cache file follow the environment variable and the
configuration file, not the option, and those with a canvaslms older than
this follow neither. An empty or misspelled CANVASLMS_CACHE_EXCLUDE does not
replace the configuration file.

The same interval decides how soon a listing is fetched again when a read asks
for something that only someone Canvas no longer lists lacks. Raising it
delays finding someone who has returned, and 0 means every such read fetches.
Each such fetch is of the whole listing, so a program that always asks for
something, such as email addresses, fetches a roster once per interval for as
long as someone who has left is in it. If Canvas can't be reached, the cached
listing is used, with a warning.

These windows can be tuned via environment variables (minutes for submissions
and name misses, days for the rest):

  CANVASLMS_SUBMISSION_TTL_MINUTES
  CANVASLMS_COURSE_CACHE_TTL_DAYS
  CANVASLMS_USER_CACHE_TTL_DAYS
  CANVASLMS_GROUP_CACHE_TTL_DAYS
  CANVASLMS_QUIZ_CACHE_TTL_DAYS
  CANVASLMS_DEFAULT_CACHE_TTL_DAYS
  CANVASLMS_MISS_REFRESH_MINUTES

Raise them to reuse the cache across more runs, or lower them to see fresher
data sooner.

CANVASLMS_MISS_REFRESH_MAX_LISTINGS and
CANVASLMS_MISS_REFRESH_MAX_LISTINGS_ALL_COURSES set the two limits on
listings; 0 turns the fetching off, without a warning, for that kind of search.

The cache file is written only by a command that sent a request to Canvas. A
command answered from the cache alone leaves the file as it was, so its
modification time tells when something was last fetched.

The attachment cache stores downloaded submission files using content-addressed
storage. Files not accessed in 90 days can be cleaned with clear-attachments.
"""
  )

  cache_subp = cache_parser.add_subparsers(
    title="cache commands",
    dest="cache_command",
    required=True
  )

  <<add cache clear subcommand>>
  <<add cache clear-attachments subcommand>>
@

