\subsection{The cache clear subcommand}

The [[cache clear]] subcommand removes the encrypted Canvas object cache.
This forces a fresh fetch of all Canvas data on the next run.

<<add cache clear subcommand>>=
clear_parser = cache_subp.add_parser(
  "clear",
  help="Clear the cached Canvas data"
)
clear_parser.set_defaults(func=clear_command)
@

The command used to require a configured hostname: with none, it aborted with
\enquote{No hostname configured, run [[canvaslms login]]}.
That turned away the very users who need it most.
Clearing the cache is a local file operation that never contacts Canvas, so
making it depend on credentials means a user who cleared their credentials, or
never had a working keyring, cannot clean up the files an earlier install left
behind---and is told to log in to delete files that logging in would not touch.

A missing hostname therefore selects the sweep from above instead of an error.
The hostname is not a permission to delete; it is only the piece of
information that narrows \emph{which} cache file to delete, and without it the
honest interpretation of [[cache clear]] is \enquote{clear the caches}.

We discard the token that [[load_credentials]] returns alongside the hostname.
Everywhere else in this module the token is the key that decrypts a cache
file, so binding it to a name here would suggest this command reads one---but
clearing only unlinks files, and never needs to open them.
Discarding it into [[_]] states that at the call site.

