  hostname, _ = canvaslms.cli.login.load_credentials(config)

  if hostname:
    <<clear the configured host's cache>>
  else:
    <<clear the caches of all hosts>>
@

Both branches print their report to standard output.
Deleting files is this command's entire effect, and it leaves nothing behind
for the user to look at afterwards, so the report \emph{is} the result---the
same role the printed listing plays for [[courses]] or [[users]].
That is the line this project draws: results go to stdout, diagnostics go to
the logger.

The reporting used to go through [[logger.info]], which put it on the wrong
side of that line.
The logger is filtered to [[WARNING]] by default, so [[canvaslms cache clear]]
printed nothing at all unless the user thought to add [[-v]]---and a command
whose only output is suppressed by default cannot be told apart from one that
silently did nothing.
The sibling [[cache clear-attachments]] already printed its report, so the two
[[cache]] subcommands disagreed about whether to say anything.

Genuine diagnostics stay with the logger: [[clear_all_caches]] still logs a
warning for each file it could not remove, because that is information about
how the sweep went rather than what the command achieved.

With a hostname we behave exactly as before.
The scheme prefix is part of the cache file name and [[login]] happily stores
a bare host name, so the hostname has to be normalized before we derive the
path.
We normalize through [[normalize_hostname]] rather than repeating the test
here: the loader ran the hostname through that same function before writing
the cache, so sharing it is what guarantees that the name we delete is the
name that was written.
<<clear the configured host's cache>>=
hostname = normalize_hostname(hostname)

if clear_cache(hostname):
  print(f"Cache cleared for {hostname}")
else:
  print(f"No cache found for {hostname}")
@

Without one we sweep, and report how many files went.
Neither branch fails when there was nothing to remove: an already-empty cache
is the state the user asked for, and scripts that clear the cache before a run
should not have to distinguish \enquote{cleared} from \enquote{already clear}.
Both outcomes are still reported, though, so that the user can tell which one
they got.
<<clear the caches of all hosts>>=
removed = clear_all_caches()

if removed:
  print(f"Cleared {removed} cache file(s) in {dirs.user_cache_dir}")
else:
  print(f"No cache files found in {dirs.user_cache_dir}")
@

Let's verify both branches, since the choice between them is the whole point
of the command's structure.
Without credentials every cache file must go; with credentials only the
configured host's file may go, even when it was configured without a scheme.

Each branch also has to say what it did, on stdout, without the user asking
for extra verbosity.
We assert that through [[capsys]] rather than [[caplog]], which is what makes
these tests notice a regression back to logger-only reporting: the default
[[WARNING]] threshold would swallow the message, and the command would go
silent again while its file-level assertions still passed.

<<test functions>>=
def test_clear_command_without_hostname_clears_all(
    tmp_cache_dir, monkeypatch, capsys):
  """Without a configured hostname, all cache files should be cleared."""
  monkeypatch.setattr(
      canvaslms.cli.login,
      "load_credentials",
      lambda config: (None, None),
  )
  write_cache_file("https://one.example.com")
  write_cache_file("https://two.example.com")

  cache.clear_command({}, None, None)

  assert list(tmp_cache_dir.iterdir()) == []
  assert "2 cache file(s)" in capsys.readouterr().out

def test_clear_command_with_hostname_spares_other_hosts(
    tmp_cache_dir, monkeypatch, capsys):
  """With a configured hostname, only that host's cache should be cleared."""
  monkeypatch.setattr(
      canvaslms.cli.login,
      "load_credentials",
      lambda config: ("one.example.com", "token"),
  )
  cleared = write_cache_file("https://one.example.com")
  spared = write_cache_file("https://two.example.com")

  cache.clear_command({}, None, None)

  assert not cleared.exists()
  assert spared.exists()
  assert "one.example.com" in capsys.readouterr().out

def test_clear_command_reports_when_there_was_nothing_to_clear(
    tmp_cache_dir, monkeypatch, capsys):
  """An empty cache directory should still produce a report, not silence."""
  monkeypatch.setattr(
      canvaslms.cli.login,
      "load_credentials",
      lambda config: (None, None),
  )

  cache.clear_command({}, None, None)

  assert "No cache files found" in capsys.readouterr().out
@

\subsection{The cache clear-attachments subcommand}

The [[cache clear-attachments]] subcommand removes cached attachment files that
haven't been accessed recently.
This helps manage disk space without requiring users to manually locate and
delete cache files.

The subcommand accepts an [[--older-than-days]] option (default: 90 days) to
control how aggressively files are removed.
A shorter period frees more space but requires more re-downloads; a longer
period keeps more files but uses more disk space.

<<add cache clear-attachments subcommand>>=
clear_attachments_parser = cache_subp.add_parser("clear-attachments",
  help="Remove old cached attachment files",
  formatter_class=argparse.RawDescriptionHelpFormatter,
  description=canvaslms.cli.utils.dedent_description("""
Removes cached attachment files that haven't been accessed in the specified
number of days. This helps manage disk space while keeping frequently-used
files available.

Cached attachments are stored in a platform-specific cache directory and use
content-addressed storage (files are identified by SHA-256 hash). This means
identical files submitted by different students or in different submission
versions are stored only once.

The cleanup process:

  1. Identifies attachments not accessed within the time window
  2. Removes their index entries
  3. Removes orphaned data files no longer referenced by any index entry
  4. Reports statistics about files removed and space freed
"""))
clear_attachments_parser.add_argument("--older-than-days",
  type=int,
  default=90,
  help="Remove files not accessed in this many days (default: 90)")
clear_attachments_parser.set_defaults(func=clear_attachments_command)
@

<<functions>>=
def clear_attachments_command(config, canvas, args):
  """Removes old cached attachments and reports statistics"""
  max_age = args.older_than_days

  print(f"Removing cached attachments not accessed in {max_age} days...")

  stats = attachment_cache.cleanup_old_attachments(max_age_days=max_age)

  files_removed = stats["files_removed"]
  bytes_freed = stats["bytes_freed"]

  # Convert bytes to human-readable format
  if bytes_freed < 1024:
    size_str = f"{bytes_freed} bytes"
  elif bytes_freed < 1024 * 1024:
    size_str = f"{bytes_freed / 1024:.1f} KB"
  elif bytes_freed < 1024 * 1024 * 1024:
    size_str = f"{bytes_freed / (1024 * 1024):.1f} MB"
  else:
    size_str = f"{bytes_freed / (1024 * 1024 * 1024):.2f} GB"

  if files_removed == 0:
    print(f"No files older than {max_age} days found.")
  else:
    print(f"Removed {files_removed} file(s), freed {size_str}.")
@

\section{Future extensions}

This module can be extended with additional cache management commands:

\begin{description}
\item[[cache stats]] Display statistics about cache usage (number of files,
  total size, oldest/newest files)
\item[[cache list]] List all cached attachments with details
\item[[cache clear-all]] Remove all caches (attachments, pickles, etc.)
\item[[cache view-location]] Display the cache directory path
\end{description}

The subcommand structure makes these additions straightforward---just add new
subparsers in new chunks.
